{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Report\n\n`rebar transition <id> in_progress closed` can print an error to stderr **with exit code 0**,\n~60s AFTER the close is already durably committed, and silently downgrade the ticket to\nclosed-WITHOUT-signature. Observed in the field: an agent reported its final `rebar\ntransition` failed with a lock error; re-reading showed the transition had LANDED.\n\n## Premise verified in current `origin/main` (cc69cc1179)\n\n`src/rebar/_commands/transition_close.py`, inside `close_ticket` (line **263**):\n\n* The STATUS event is committed and the lock released BEFORE the signing tail runs.\n* Line **433** calls `sign_completion_verdict(...)`, whose append goes through\n  `write_and_push` -> `event_append` -> a fresh 60s `write_lock` acquisition.\n* Line **434** catches `_signing.SigningError` and lines **439-445** write\n  `Warning: closed <id> WITHOUT a completion signature — <exc.message> ...` to stderr and\n  fall through. `close_ticket` then returns its normal success payload (lines **492-498**:\n  `ticket_id`, `from`, `to`, `newly_unblocked`, `noop`) and the command exits **0**.\n* The returned payload carries **no field at all** about the signature outcome, so no\n  library, MCP or CLI consumer can detect the loss programmatically.\n* The sibling drift refusal (lines **421-429**) lands unsigned the same way, likewise with\n  no payload field.\n\nThe `except` comment (lines **435-438**) justifies the exit-0 degrade for a *missing\nssh-keygen* — a permanent, operator-fixable condition. That rationale does not transfer to a\n**transient lock timeout**, which is what the field incident hit, and the raw appended\n`exc.message` (\"flock: could not acquire lock after 60s\") reads as the CLOSE having failed\nwhen the close in fact succeeded.\n\nNote: bug royal-weariless-zebrafish (merged cc69cc1179) added bounded retry at the\n`event_append` write lock, making the signature append far likelier to survive contention.\nThat MITIGATES frequency; it does not fix the reporting shape.\n\n## Direction\n\nMake the loss **machine-readable, honestly worded, and durably detectable** — without\nchanging the process exit code.\n\n1. **Payload marker.** `close_ticket` returns an additional\n   `completion_signature: {\"signed\": bool, \"cause\": str, \"error\": str}`, mirroring the\n   machine-branchable `PlanReviewAttestation.as_dict()` idiom already used for exactly this\n   problem in `src/rebar/llm/plan_review/resign.py:457`, so a caller branches on `cause`\n   without parsing English. The `cause` vocabulary is **closed and total** — every close-path\n   outcome has exactly one value, and `error` is the empty string wherever there is no\n   failure text:\n\n   | outcome | `signed` | `cause` | `error` |\n   |---|---|---|---|\n   | signature persisted | `True` | `signed` | `\"\"` |\n   | `SigningError` at line 434 | `False` | `sign_failed` | `exc.message` |\n   | drift refusal at line 421 | `False` | `material_drifted` | `\"\"` |\n   | `--force` close (signing deliberately skipped) | `False` | `force_bypassed` | `\"\"` |\n\n   A non-close transition (e.g. `open -> in_progress`) carries no such block at all; the key\n   is present only on the close path.\n\n   **Consumers that must forward it.** `close_ticket`'s payload is re-projected field by field\n   at two boundaries that would otherwise silently strip the new key, so both are in the edit\n   set: `src/rebar/_lib_writes.py:414-419` (the public `rebar.transition()` return) and\n   `src/rebar/_commands/transition.py:580-591` (the CLI `--output json` branch). MCP\n   `transition_ticket` (`src/rebar/_mcp_writes.py:190-195`) forwards the library result and so\n   inherits it once `_lib_writes` does — asserted, not assumed. The shared\n   `src/rebar/schemas/transition_result.schema.json` gains `completion_signature` as an\n   OPTIONAL property (it stays out of `required` because `reopen` shares the schema and has no\n   close path); the schema already sets `additionalProperties: true`, so this is additive.\n2. **Honest message.** The stderr text leads with the fact that the close **LANDED** and only\n   the signature did not, then names the recovery — instead of appending a bare lock error\n   that describes the succeeded operation as failed.\n3. **Durable detection.** Best-effort audit comment on the ticket recording\n   closed-without-signature and its cause, mirroring the existing FORCE_CLOSE audit comment\n   the same function already writes (line **473** region). Best-effort and swallowed on\n   failure, exactly like that sibling — it must never wedge a close that already committed.\n\n### Why NOT a nonzero exit code (deliberate, recorded)\n\nThe ticket's report offers \"a nonzero exit **or at minimum** a machine-readable failure\nmarker\". The exit path is rejected here for two concrete reasons:\n\n* `docs/exit-codes.md` declares the contract **frozen** as of the 2026-06-09 breaking-change\n  window; it is pinned by `tests/interfaces/lifecycle/test_exit_codes.py`, and the table at\n  `docs/exit-codes.md:158` fixes `transition` at 0 success / 1 error / 10 mismatch. A NEW\n  code is additive-only in a future window — a release decision, not an implementation one.\n* Reusing exit **1** would be actively harmful: agents read 1 as \"the transition failed\" and\n  re-run it, which then exits **10** (status mismatch, already closed). That trades a silent\n  loss for a louder wrong answer.\n\nThe marker route satisfies the reporting need within the frozen contract. Raising a new exit\ncode is left to a contract window and is explicitly out of scope here.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A close whose completion-signature append raises `SigningError` returns a payload with\n      `completion_signature.signed == False` and a `cause` naming the failure — verified by a\n      test that forces the append to fail and asserts the payload\n- [ ] A successful close returns `completion_signature` `{signed: True, cause: \"signed\",\n      error: \"\"}` — verified by a test\n- [ ] A `--force` close returns `signed == False` with `cause == \"force_bypassed\"` — verified\n      by a test\n- [ ] The marker survives to the public consumers: `rebar.transition()` returns it, and\n      `rebar transition … --output json` prints it — verified by a test per surface\n- [ ] The stderr text on that failure states the close LANDED and only the signature did not,\n      and does not present the lock error as the close failing — verified by a test asserting\n      the message text\n- [ ] The closed-without-signature state is detectable by a later reader via a durable record\n      on the ticket — verified by a test reading the ticket after such a close\n- [ ] The material-drift refusal path reports `signed == False` with\n      `cause == \"material_drifted\"` — verified by a test\n- [ ] Exit code is unchanged (0) on all of the above — verified by a test, pinning the\n      deliberate decision above"}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786559106075219001,"uuid":"e6165f70-7d52-4fdf-b7de-1b843337ede4"}